WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.320
From the space station to the Red Planet, NASA's missions continue moving forward.

00:04.320 --> 00:07.440
Here's what's new this week in your NASA Minute.

00:07.440 --> 00:12.800
On Thursday, NASA officially welcomed Matt Anderson as the agency's 16th Deputy Administrator,

00:12.800 --> 00:15.920
following his confirmation by the U.S. Senate earlier in the week.

00:15.920 --> 00:21.280
A retired U.S. Air Force Colonel with decades of experience in space operations and national security,

00:21.280 --> 00:25.760
Anderson will help lead NASA's missions in exploration, science, and aeronautics as the

00:25.760 --> 00:30.080
agency pushes toward the moon, Mars, and beyond. Welcome to NASA, Matt.

00:31.040 --> 00:36.080
This week, NASA's Psyche spacecraft flew past Mars using the planet's gravity to slingshot itself

00:36.080 --> 00:39.680
deeper into the solar system on its journey to study the asteroid Psyche.

00:40.560 --> 00:44.240
Stunning new images of the Red Planet were captured, including views of craters,

00:44.240 --> 00:46.800
the planet's horizon, and its South Polar ice cap.

00:48.320 --> 00:53.040
A SpaceX Dragon spacecraft successfully arrived at the International Space Station this week,

00:53.120 --> 00:59.280
carrying over 6,000 pounds of supplies and scientific research for the Expedition 74 crew.

00:59.280 --> 01:03.440
Among the experiments delivered were studies focused on astronaut health, space weather,

01:03.440 --> 01:08.400
and Earth observation, continuing more than 25 years of scientific research aboard the

01:08.400 --> 01:13.280
orbiting laboratory. Coming up next week, NASA will provide a moon-based update.

01:13.280 --> 01:18.400
Be sure to tune in to hear more about our progress in establishing humanity's first lunar outpost.

01:19.120 --> 01:22.480
Whether in orbit, on the way to an asteroid, or preparing for the moon,

01:22.480 --> 01:26.240
NASA's mission of exploration and discovery continues every day.

01:26.240 --> 01:34.320
We'll see you next time on The NASA Minute.

